# Communication Layers

## Renderer

- Renderer service calls use `window.pairApi`.
- Electron-native operations use `window.windowApi`.
- Renderer code under `src/ui/` must not import from `src/electron/`.
- Shared request, response, and push types belong under `src/shared/types/`.

## Electron main

- Preload forwards logical service calls through the `service-bridge:*` IPC
  envelope.
- `handleServiceBridgeInvoke` is the service dispatch for renderer clients.
- `ModularSupervisor` communicates with `nvpair-ui-broker` through
  `nvpair-service`, over newline JSON-RPC on a local socket or named pipe.
- Electron starts only `nvpair-service`, and only if it is not already running.
  The service owns the broker, and the broker owns every worker process.
- Modular notifications update `ModularBridgeState`, which emits typed renderer
  push events.

## Terminal client

- The `nvpair` command on PATH launches the bundled Go `nvpair-tui`, which
  attaches to `nvpair-service` like Electron does.
- Electron generates that launcher on packaged builds
  (`src/electron/nvpair-command.ts`).
- There is no Electron console client. The one local control socket is
  `nvpair-service`'s per-user endpoint; do not add another.

## Backend network traffic

- Node discovery, pairing, trust, workload propagation, error synchronization,
  and routed inference are backend-owned.
- PAIR must not implement a parallel node-to-node protocol.
- Clustered backend peer channels use the trust and transport selected by the
  backend services; PAIR relays control-plane requests through the broker.
- Public inference clients connect to local Ollama- or OpenAI-compatible proxy
  endpoints.
- Ask-to-join admission requests travel through `pairApi`, the existing local
  service endpoint and the generic broker `cluster:*` relay. Cluster-manager
  owns eligibility, source/rate/capacity limits, expiry, explicit safe-solo
  replacement and the existing durable pairing transaction. No TypeScript
  pairing HTTP, membership decision or teardown is permitted.
- `cluster:get-join-requests` and `cluster:join-requests-changed` carry the same
  full snapshot independently of `ClusterInitialSnapshot`; hydrate on reconnect.
  Request IDs and exact child invite IDs are distinct. Claimed names/UUIDs are
  unverified and snapshots contain no PIN/security material. Incoming requests
  remain passive review data with no automatic modal or focus change.
- Only a local member client may approve, choosing authority over that device.
  The member stays EAP Server/inviter and retrieves/displays the PIN locally via
  the exact pending `cluster:invite-status`; the requester stays EAP Peer/joiner
  and enters it with its own grant choice through `cluster:respond-to-invite`.
  Approval or HTTP status is not durable membership. No fallback Invite for an
  unsupported peer, cluster merge or automatic acceptance is allowed.
- The unauthenticated source-bound `POST /v1/cluster/join-requests` prelude
  belongs on the existing plain cluster listener. Its private IPv4/ULA/Tailscale
  CGNAT source policy, fixture loopback allowance and bounded abuse controls
  are specified in `services/nvpair-cluster-manager/spec.md` §7.0.1; no new
  transport/listener/firewall permission or peer approval/grant API is added.
  Client detach preserves work; manager restart loses requests. Re-read actual
  membership after lost status before retrying. Old local method-not-found is
  unsupported; a failed connection never establishes eligibility.
- Cluster-manager alone writes target-local, admission-bound remote-management
  grants. Local invite initiation and acceptance require `allowRemoteManagement`;
  each side chooses authority over its own device. The local grant list/setter
  and change push travel through the existing service/broker cluster relay;
  never expose them through a peer endpoint or trust gossip.
- Engine-manager classifies every `ec` route as read or mutation after verifying
  the mTLS caller. Mutations require a fresh shared
  `clustertrust.Mesh.RemoteManagement` allow; broker-owned writes recheck at
  acceptance under their existing locks. No UI readback or payload caller grants
  authority. Preserve `-32012/remote_management_denied`, independently of
  `-32011/local_veto` and `-32010` conflict. Reads and permitted inference remain
  available; revocation does not undo already accepted work.

## Documentation

When changing IPC channels, logical service channels, push events, process
ownership, security flows, or runtime ports, update:

- `docs/architecture.md`;
- `docs/frontend-api.md`;
- `docs/services-backend.md`;
- the relevant `.cursor/rules` files.
